    "module":  "hepatobiliary",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Acute cholecystitis is inflammation of the gallbladder, most commonly caused by obstruction of the cystic duct by a gallstone (calculous cholecystitis, 90–95%) with subsequent bacterial infection and distension. Acalculous cholecystitis (5–10%) occurs in critically ill patients. The classic presentation is severe, constant right upper quadrant or epigastric pain radiating to the right scapula or shoulder tip, with fever, nausea, and a positive Murphy\u0027s sign (inspiratory arrest during deep RUQ palpation). Jaundice may occur if there is concurrent choledocholithiasis. Complications include empyema, perforation, and gangrenous cholecystitis.",
